---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import { href } from '../../utils/url'
import {
  summary,
  denominator,
  repro,
  anisotropy,
  geometry,
  perLayer,
  geometryRefs,
  primaryOverlap,
  models,
  shortName
} from '../../data/research'

const title = 'Removing the refusal direction from four models'
const description =
  'Reproducing single-direction refusal ablation paper, and then asking whether the damage to maths is real or an artefact. Draft!'

const num = (s: string | number, dp = 1) => Number(s).toFixed(dp)
const pp = (s: string) => (Number(s) > 0 ? '+' : '') + Number(s).toFixed(1)

const refusal = models.map((m) => ({
  model: m,
  mk: summary.find((r) => r.model === m && r.task === 'refusal (marker)')!,
  cf: summary.find((r) => r.model === m && r.task === 'refusal (clf)')!
}))

const mathRows = models.map((m) => ({
  model: m,
  gsm8k: summary.find((r) => r.model === m && r.task === 'gsm8k')!,
  svamp: summary.find((r) => r.model === m && r.task === 'svamp')!,
  mmlu: summary.find((r) => r.model === m && r.task === 'mmlu')!
}))

const estimators = [...new Set(geometry.map((g) => g.estimator))].map((est) => {
  const rows = geometry.filter((g) => g.estimator === est)
  const range = (k: 'energy_reduced' | 'energy_whitened') => {
    const v = rows.map((r) => Number(r[k]))
    return `${Math.min(...v).toFixed(3)}–${Math.max(...v).toFixed(3)}`
  }
  return { est, eRaw: range('energy_reduced'), eWhit: range('energy_whitened') }
})

const chance = geometryRefs[0].null_reduced
const verdict = (m: string) =>
  m.startsWith('Qwen')
    ? 'yes'
    : m.startsWith('granite')
      ? 'partly'
      : m.startsWith('Phi')
        ? 'no'
        : 'could not test'
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="wrap paper-head">
    <a class="back-link" href={href()}>&larr; All papers</a>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="meta">
      <span class="tag" style="--h:200">My own work</span>
      <span class="sep">·</span>
      <span>pilot run, {repro.date.slice(0, 10)}</span>
      <span class="sep">·</span>
      <span>{repro.models.length} models</span>
      <span class="sep">·</span>
      <span>seed {repro.seed}</span>
      <span class="sep">·</span>
      <span>{repro.gpu}</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="prose prose--paper">
      <h2>What I did</h2>

      <p>
        I started by reproducing <a href={href('papers/refusal-single-direction')}
        >Arditi et al.</a> on four new small open models. Find the direction that carries
        refusal, remove it, and check that the model stops refusing on few benchmarks.
      </p>

      <p>
        Then I wanted to test a claim that gets repeated: that removing refusal
        breaks the model at maths (or in general). The usual evidence is a drop on GSM8K.
      </p>

      <p>
        BUT! A GSM8K drop does not prove maths was hit. GSM8K is a long chain of steps. If
        a model gets slightly worse at everything, the errors pile up along the chain, and
        GSM8K falls further than any short task. So a drop there is equally consistent with <em>maths was damaged</em> and with <em>the model was damaged and GSM8K noticed
        first</em>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Separating those two needs something extra: a non-maths task with equally long
        answers.
      </p>

      <p>
        I also measured the geometry directly, since the claim that refusal and maths
        overlap is a claim about angles between subspaces, and one can check it without
        running any attack.
      </p>

      <h2>Method</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>The direction.</strong> Difference-in-means. Run harmful and harmless
        prompts, take the mean activation of each at every layer, subtract. Pick the layer
        where the gap is largest. 128 prompts per side.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>The ablation.</strong> Applied as reversible forward hooks at inference (altering on the run),
        weights unchanged. That is not the same thing as a released abliterated
        model, which also edits the embedding matrix.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Refusal, measured twice.</strong> Once by looking for phrases like
        &ldquo;I cannot&rdquo;, once with a trained classifier. Both, because the phrase
        test is known to be badly biased - in one published comparison it reported 72%
        where the classifier reported 96%.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Capability.</strong> GSM8K (long chains tasks) and SVAMP (short chains tasks) for
        maths, MMLU as a control. Every number has a bootstrap confidence interval, paired
        where the items are paired.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>A cost denominator.</strong> KL divergence per token and perplexity on
        ordinary text, so capability changes can be read against how much the edit
        disturbed the model in general.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Geometry.</strong> Principal angles between the refusal subspace and a
        maths subspace, rank 6. Measured three ways: in the full space, in a top-100 PCA
        space, and in that same PCA space after whitening with Ledoit&ndash;Wolf shrinkage.
        The middle one exists to separate the effect of the metric from the effect of
        reducing dimensions — otherwise those two are confounded.
      </p>

      <p>
        Harmful prompts are used as counts only. Generations from them are reduced to
        true/false in memory and never printed or saved. No ablated weights were produced.
      </p>

      <h2>The reproduction worked on one model out of four</h2>

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Model</th>
              <th>Refusal, phrase test</th>
              <th>Refusal, classifier</th>
              <th>Worked?</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              refusal.map(({ model, mk, cf }) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{shortName(model)}</td>
                  <td>
                    {num(mk.clean, 0)}% &rarr; {num(mk.abl, 0)}%
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    {num(cf.clean, 0)}% &rarr; {num(cf.abl, 0)}%
                  </td>
                  <td>{verdict(model)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>Qwen worked.</strong> Refusal fell from 99% to 17% on the classifier. That
        is the result the paper describes.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Granite only partly worked.</strong> The two measures disagree by 43
        points. The phrase test says refusal collapsed from 72% to 9%. The classifier says
        it went from 95% to 75%.
      </p>

      <h2>The two failures</h2>

      <h3>SmolLM3 had nothing to remove</h3>

      <p>
        Before I touched it, SmolLM3 refused 2% of harmful prompts on the phrase test and
        8% on the classifier. There was no refusal behaviour there to remove? Wanted to check on this later - may be an artefact or a bug.
      </p>

      <h3>Phi lost accuracy without losing refusal</h3>

      <p>
        Same with Phi. Refusal stayed at 99% on both measures.
        Nothing was removed. But MMLU fell by 3.5 points, and that was the only
        statistically significant control change in the whole run.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the edit did damage and did not do its job.
      </p>

      <h2>A problem with my layer choice</h2>

      <p>
        I think I know why the failures happened, and it is my fault rather than the
        method&rsquo;s.
      </p>

      <p>
        I picked the layer where the harmful/harmless gap is largest. Arditi et al. do not
        do that. They search layers <em>and</em> token positions, score candidates on
        whether ablation removes refusal, whether adding the direction causes refusal, and
        whether harmless behaviour stays put and they throw away any layer past 80% of
        the network depth. The reason for that cutoff is specific: near the output you stop
        finding the representation that decides to refuse and start finding the tokens a
        refusal is spelled with.
      </p>

      <p>My chosen layers landed.</p>

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Model</th>
              <th>Layer chosen</th>
              <th>Depth</th>
              <th>Inside Arditi&rsquo;s cutoff?</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              geometryRefs.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{shortName(r.model)}</td>
                  <td>
                    {r.layer} of {r.n_layers}
                  </td>
                  <td>{((r.layer / r.n_layers) * 100).toFixed(0)}%</td>
                  <td class="sig-cell">no</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="todo">
        <p>
          <strong>To do.</strong> Sanity check. Writing. Plus if I have time I'd like to try to find math direction with DIM method correctly this time.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Does removing refusal break maths?</h2>

      <p>Change in accuracy, in percentage points, with 95% intervals.</p>

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Model</th>
              <th>GSM8K (long chain)</th>
              <th>SVAMP (short chain)</th>
              <th>MMLU (control)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              mathRows.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{shortName(r.model)}</td>
                  {[r.gsm8k, r.svamp, r.mmlu].map((c) => (
                    <td class={c.sig === 'True' ? 'sig-cell' : ''}>
                      {pp(c.delta)}
                      <span class="ci">
                        {' '}
                        ({pp(c.lo)}, {pp(c.hi)})
                      </span>
                    </td>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Nothing moved. And the edit was gentle by the cost measures too.</p>

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Model</th>
              <th>KL per token</th>
              <th>Perplexity, before &rarr; after</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              denominator.map((d) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{shortName(d.model)}</td>
                  <td>{num(d.kl_token, 3)}</td>
                  <td>
                    {num(d.ppl_clean, 2)} &rarr; {num(d.ppl_abl, 2)} (&times;
                    {num(d.ppl_ratio, 3)})
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>


      <h2>What the geometry says</h2>

      <p>
        Two <em>random</em> 6-dimensional
        subspaces of a 1536-dimensional space are almost perpendicular already, sitting
        around 86 to 88 degrees. So &ldquo;the angle is 70 degrees&rdquo; is not
        near-orthogonality and not a null result. It is far below chance, which means real
        alignment.
      </p>

      <p>
        Every number below is therefore reported against chance measured in the same space.
        In the reduced space that chance level is <strong>{chance.toFixed(3)}</strong>.
      </p>

      <h3>The space is very uneven</h3>

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Model</th>
              <th>Dimensions</th>
              <th>Participation ratio</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              anisotropy.map((a) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{shortName(a.model)}</td>
                  <td>{a.d}</td>
                  <td>{a.participation_ratio.toFixed(1)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        These models have 1536 to 3072 dimensions. The participation ratio is a rough count
        of how many are doing real work. It is between 8 and 13. A space with thousands of
        dimensions behaves like one with about ten. That is <a href={href('papers/contextual-anisotropy')}>anisotropy</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>The uncorrected overlap number is meaningless</h3>

      <p>
        The strongest thing in this run is a control, not a result. I built a subspace out
        of harmless prompts versus other harmless prompts. It contains no concept at all.
        Is it noise? WIP.
      </p>

      <p>
        That noise subspace overlaps the refusal subspace by {Math.min(
          ...geometryRefs.map((r) => r.harmless_noise)
        ).toFixed(2)} to {Math.max(...geometryRefs.map((r) => r.harmless_noise)).toFixed(2)}.
        The maths subspace overlaps it by {Math.min(
          ...primaryOverlap.map((p) => p.reduced)
        ).toFixed(2)} to {Math.max(...primaryOverlap.map((p) => p.reduced)).toFixed(2)}.
      </p>

      <p>
        Those are the same. In the model&rsquo;s own coordinates, pure noise looks as
        &ldquo;overlapping&rdquo; with refusal as maths does. Any published overlap number
        computed this way, without that control, is not evidence of anything.
      </p>

    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .draft-badge {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem;
    border: 1px dashed var(--border-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--bg-sunken);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .todo {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--border-strong);
    background: var(--bg-sunken);
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem 0.9rem 1.1rem;
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .todo :global(p:first-child),
  .todo :global(ul) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .todo :global(p:last-child),
  .todo :global(li:last-child) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .todo :global(ul) {
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }

  .todo :global(li) + :global(li) {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  .ci {
    color: var(--text-faint);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .sig-cell {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
